      <h1>Correction: gate 2.1, vexes 0.3, mcp-librarian 3.1</h1>
      <div class="blog-post-meta">
        <span class="blog-post-date">Originally August 12, 2026 · corrected August 28, 2026</span>
        <span class="blog-post-tag">correction</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="blog-article-lede">The original post tried to make an honesty point while still overstating several properties. This version records the releases without preserving guarantees the current implementations do not support.</p>

    <div class="blog-body">
      <h2>Gate 2.1.0</h2>
      <p>The release corrected rule loading/counting, added or refined redacted reporting, and made credential verification explicitly opt-in. The current generated catalog reports {{ versions.gate.rules }} rules. That number describes loaded patterns, not detection quality.</p>
      <p>Default scans are local, but “no network” is too broad: verification sends a credential to its provider, and update operations can contact remote sources. Core source rewrites use a temporary-file-and-rename path; not every Gate state or configuration write shares that guarantee. Some subprocess paths use argument arrays and some are shell-backed.</p>

      <h2>vexes 0.3.0</h2>
      <p>This was a historical release in a project now at v{{ versions.vexes.sourceVersion }}. CVSS parsing, model-assisted explanation, SARIF, and GitHub Action behavior evolved over subsequent releases. Old demo counts were point-in-time OSV observations and should not be repeated as a stable benchmark.</p>
      <p>Current AI support is pluggable across configured OpenAI-compatible and Anthropic-compatible endpoints. Model output is a draft. SARIF fingerprints are intended to help cross-run correlation; deduplication ultimately depends on the receiving platform. Vexes contacts OSV and registries during normal use, so it runs locally but is not an offline scanner.</p>

      <h2>mcp-librarian 3.1.0</h2>
      <p>The release added signing to supported write paths, SSRF mitigations to current fetch helpers, content screening, and install/reload fixes. Those are useful controls, but the original post collapsed detection into enforcement.</p>
      <p>The current store records <code>VERIFIED</code>, <code>TAMPERED</code>, or <code>UNSIGNED</code> while indexing. Current <code>load_skill</code> and <code>load_section</code> responses do not enforce refusal of tampered content or reliably attach that status. Clients must check and reject it separately. Pattern-based content screening and SSRF controls also reduce risk without proving fetched material safe.</p>

      <h2>Current rule for claims</h2>
      <p>A feature claim should identify its scope, evidence, network route, incomplete state, and important bypass. Measured numbers need commands, inputs, dates, and raw artifacts. A control that reports a problem is not automatically a control that blocks it.</p>
      <p>All three projects execute locally and may contact documented upstream services for requested features. None has had an external efficacy or security audit. Their output is evidence to review, not a guarantee.</p>

